WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.900
Why are I beams shaped like an I?

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.359
Have you ever actually taken a moment to think
about this?

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:09.530
This simple technology forms the basis of
all our largest structures and even features

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.050
in one of the world’s most famous photos,
but after asking some of my friends few actually

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.040
knew the answer to this simple question.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.630
The I beam is designed in that way to handle
a maximum bending load while using the least

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:21.630
amount of material.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.270
Let’s look at an I beam supported on either
end to understand more.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:29.361
When we apply a uniform load across this beam
the max deflection will occur here in the

00:00:29.361 --> 00:00:30.361
middle.

00:00:30.361 --> 00:00:32.820
We can calculate the deflection with this
equation.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.309
This may look complicated, but it really isn’t.

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:40.629
W represents the uniformly distributed load
in terms of Newtons per metre, l is the span

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:45.379
between supports, E is the youngs modulus
which as explained in my Material Properties

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:48.699
101 video describes the stiffness of the material.

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:51.140
But the variable we want to focus on is this.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.459
I represents the second moment of area sometimes
called the moment area of inertia.

00:00:56.459 --> 00:01:00.329
This describes the shape of the beam, more
specifically it describes how the material

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:02.569
is distributed throughout the shape.

00:01:02.569 --> 00:01:07.110
These two shapes have the same area, but that
area is distributed very differently and that

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.110
is important.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.930
A see-saw is a good analogy for this idea.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:15.050
When we place weight in the middle, it is
very easy to lift, in fact if it is placed

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.570
exactly over the middle, we aren’t lifting
it at all.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.601
but the further we move that weight to the
end, the more difficult it is to lift, due

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:22.870
to the increasing leverage.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.210
A very similar thing happens with beams in
bending.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.710
Material at the centre of the beam, which
is called the neutral axis, does not resist

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.990
bending and the material furthest away from
the centre,resists the bending the most.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.950
It is called the neutral axis because if we
place a bending load downward the same way

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:42.050
we did before, the beam will bend in a way
that will cause the lower edge to be in max

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:47.050
tension and the upper edge to be in max compression
and the values of stress gradually decrease

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:51.460
to 0 at the neutral axis where there is neither
tension or compression.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.590
Because the tension and compression is maximum
the furthest from neutral axis we want to

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:00.300
maximise the amount of material on the outside
of the profile where it is needed most

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.040
The more material further from the neutral
axis the larger the second moment of area

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.240
will be.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.310
Applying that to the equation we can see that
a larger second moment of area will result

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.849
in a smaller deflection.

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:14.819
So if we to place this section under the same
bending load, it would actually be stronger

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:19.290
if we flipped it over 90 degrees, because
more material now located future from the

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.719
neutral axis.

00:02:20.719 --> 00:02:24.859
We could make it even stronger again, by reducing
this thickness to a minimum just enough to

00:02:24.859 --> 00:02:29.489
resist the shear stress and placing that material
at the the top and suddenly we are back to

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:30.879
the I beam shape.

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:33.579
You can see this idea put into action all
around you.

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:37.549
In my last video I mentioned just one of them
when I spoke about the Willis Tower using

00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:39.349
a bundled tube structure.

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.129
This structure maximises the amount of steel
on the outside of the building to maximise

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.370
it’s resistance to lateral bending from
wind and other loads.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.480
I will be talking about another application
of this technology in my next video and if

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.010
you can think of any other examples of the
second moment of area being applied in the

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.970
world around you be sure to share it in the
comments.

